# gnupg + xclip: the system binaries OUR shipped apps shell out to. gnupg provides `gpg`,
# used by BOTH the `passwords` command (packages/passwords/ -- encrypts/decrypts the store
# at ~/Vault/passwords.txt.gpg, AES256, the master password is the passphrase) AND the
# `backup` command (packages/backup/ -- encrypts the home-directory archive
# ~/backup_<date>.tar.gz.gpg, AES256). xclip copies a selected password to the X11
# clipboard (passwords). gnupg is normally present only transitively (pacman/base-devel
# pull it in), so it is named EXPLICITLY here since shipped apps hard-depend on it. xclip
# is also generally useful on the desktop (the user's own clip-it workflow).
gnupg
xclip
# xdotool: X11 automation CLI (simulate keys/clicks, find/activate/move windows by name or
# class). A general-purpose scripting utility for the OpenBox desktop -- useful for user
# automation and for driving/inspecting GUI apps from the shell.
xdotool
# rclone: the ONE extra system binary the OPTIONAL cloud backup uses. `backup`
# (packages/backup/) copies its encrypted archives to Google Drive ONLY when the user has
# opted in via `azzio backup --configure` (packages/azzio/backup_targets.py); with the default
# (disabled) config rclone is never invoked. Shipped so the opt-in works out of the box
# (rclone stores its own OAuth token under ~/.config/rclone). NOT from the AUR.
rclone
# qemu-full + edk2-ovmf + virt-viewer: the system binaries the `hypervisor` command
# (packages/hypervisor/) shells out to. It is a HOST-side tool -- run `hypervisor` inside a
# directory and it spins up a per-directory QEMU/KVM VM. qemu-full provides
# `qemu-system-x86_64` (runs the VM) and `qemu-img` (creates the qcow2 disk); edk2-ovmf
# provides the OVMF UEFI firmware (OVMF_CODE / the OVMF_VARS NVRAM template the VM boots
# from); virt-viewer provides `remote-viewer` (the SPICE display window). Named explicitly
# here because the shipped app hard-depends on them (none is pulled in by the baseline: the
# releng image ships only the GUEST agents qemu-guest-agent/spice-vdagent, not the host
# emulator). `pgrep` (the running-state check) comes from procps-ng in base. NOT from the AUR.
qemu-full
edk2-ovmf
virt-viewer
