# xrdb: loads ~/.Xresources (Xft.dpi + Xcursor.size) at session start (~/.xinitrc) -- the GLOBAL
# SCALE backbone every X client reads. `azzio display scale` also re-runs `xrdb -merge` to
# re-apply a scale change live. Without this package the whole fractional UI scale (e.g. 1.35)
# silently does not take effect (the .xinitrc xrdb line is guarded, so it just no-ops).
xorg-xrdb
# spice-vdagent: the SPICE guest agent. The releng baseline ships open-vm-tools (VMware),
# qemu-guest-agent and virtualbox-guest-utils, but NOT spice-vdagent -- a clear gap for the
# QEMU/KVM guest the ISO is tested on, which is accessed over SPICE (verified: the
# /dev/virtio-ports/com.redhat.spice.0 channel is present, but the agent was absent). It is the
# standard SPICE guest-integration piece: with spice-vdagentd (the daemon that reads that
# channel) + spice-vdagent (the session agent), the guest keeps the pointer in sync with the
# SPICE client ("client mouse mode": no grab, no lag), auto-fits the resolution, and shares the
# clipboard. It is the correct, low-risk fix for the reported pointer regression (no hover /
# dropped clicks / stale labels are the classic symptoms of a SPICE guest whose pointer is not
# agent-coordinated). NOTE: the test VM also enumerates BOTH a "QEMU Virtio Tablet" (absolute)
# AND an emulated PS/2 mouse (relative); a relative+absolute pointer pair can itself make the
# cursor erratic, so if symptoms persist after the agent the next lever is the host device model
# (drop the PS/2 mouse, keep the virtio-tablet) -- that is a HOST/QEMU config the ISO cannot set,
# so it is documented rather than forced (disabling a PS/2 device guest-side would break real
# PS/2 hardware). Harmless everywhere: spice-vdagent exits at once with no SPICE channel.
# spice-vdagentd is enabled in _link_services; spice-vdagent is started from the OpenBox session
# autostart (it needs a running X session).
spice-vdagent
xorg-xsetroot
# xset: `azzio`'s OpenBox autostart runs `xset r rate 300 25` to shorten the keyboard
# autorepeat delay, so HOLDING an FN volume/brightness key ramps quickly (each repeat is one
# step). Without this package `xset` is absent and that (guarded) line is a silent no-op.
xorg-xset
# The X client libraries the compiled media OSD (azzio-osd) links: libx11 (Xlib), libxrandr
# (primary-monitor geometry for the bottom-middle placement), libxft (anti-aliased percent text,
# matching the old tkinter look). They are normally pulled in transitively by xorg-server/kitty,
# but the OSD hard-links them, so they are named EXPLICITLY here (and are the build-host -dev
# deps too -- see terminal_user_interface_build.TERMINAL_USER_INTERFACE_BUILD_DEPS).
libx11
libxrandr
libxft
libxcomposite
libxdamage
libxrender
picom
xcb-util-cursor
mesa
feh
gvfs
xdg-user-dirs
xdg-utils
kitty
